#X text 11 9 The "Z-plane" also refers to the "Z Transform" and is a complex plane with a real (horizontal) and imaginary (vertical) axis. It plots Poles (with an "X") and Zeros (with "O") whose location represents the frequency and the coefficcient's amplitude., f 64;
#X obj 772 66 hsl 128 15 20 22050 0 0 \$0-hip xxx empty -2 -8 0 10 #dcdcdc #000000 #000000 0 1;
#X text 797 65 [hip~] Hz;
#X text 11 154 You can interpret it with a "Polar" diagram \, where the amplitude represents the Pole/Zero coefficient and the angle represent the frequency. As we know \, a Pole is a resonance that boosts a frequency and a Zero is an anti resonance that attenuates (or zeros) a frequency. In this example \, the value of the pole goes from 0 to 1 for both [lop~] and [hip~] \, while the zero has a value of "1" for [hip~]. Since the frequency of the pole or zero is defined by the angle \, we can see that the angle is 0 and that means the frequency is also 0 hz! Note the Z Plane has a circle \, which is the unitary circle \, with a radius/amplitude of "1"!, f 64;
#X obj 413 389 vsl 22 157 0 1.1 0 0 \$0-gain-send \$0-gain empty 0 -9 0 10 #404040 #fc0400 #000000 0 1;
#X obj 420 437 cnv 5 5 5 empty empty G 0 0 0 14 #404040 #fcfcfc 0;
#X obj 420 453 cnv 5 5 5 empty empty A 0 0 0 14 #404040 #fcfcfc 0;
#X obj 420 469 cnv 5 5 5 empty empty I 0 0 0 14 #404040 #fcfcfc 0;
#X obj 420 489 cnv 5 5 5 empty empty N 0 0 0 14 #404040 #fcfcfc 0;
#X text 11 300 So \, for [lop~] \, as the pole coefficient reaches 1 \, the lower frequencies get a boost \, which is compensated by the gain parameter \, turning it into a low pass filter. Since the gain is not plotted in the plane \, we have a slider just for that at the bottom lft., f 64;
#X floatatom 413 552 5 0 0 0 - - - 0;
#X text 11 465 Change the sliders on the top right to set the frequency that corresponds both the [lop~] or the [hip~] filter. The coefficients are calculated as before and you can see the Z-Plane plot as well as the frequency response below., f 51;
#X text 11 74 ELSE has [zbiplot] that plots a biquad system (2-Poles/2-Zeros) into the Z-Plane. You can feed it the coordinates of Poles and Zeroes or the biquad coefficients directly (we'll see more about the conversion from and to Poles/Zeros later in this section). We're using it here to plot [lop~] \, [hip~] and [lop2~]., f 64;
#X obj 785 88 hsl 128 15 20 22050 1 0 \$0-lop3 xxx empty -2 -8 0 10 #dcdcdc #000000 #000000 0 1;
#X text 810 87 [lop3~] Hz;
